      "pattern_id": "P1",
      "M": "Let e be an entity and let m be an attribute defined over time. Let v(e, m, t) denote the value or state of entity e with respect to attribute m at time t.\n\nA trajectory shift pattern holds when there exists a time or event τ such that the behavior of v(e, m, t) differs before and after τ.\n\nLet g_pre denote the trend of v(e, m, t) before τ, and let g_post denote the trend of v(e, m, t) after τ. A trajectory shift holds when:\n\ng_pre ≠ g_post\n\nor more strongly,\n\n|g_post - g_pre| > ε,\n\nwhere ε is a threshold for meaningful change.",
      "T": "A trajectory shift pattern is a temporal pattern in which an entity changes its direction, speed, or status after a specific time or event. The entity may shift from slow growth to rapid growth, growth to stagnation, growth to decline, decline to recovery, dominance to slowdown, niche activity to momentum, or one technological pathway to another. The pattern requires an explicit before/after structure or a stated turning point; a simple statement that something is growing, declining, dominant, or important is not sufficient.",
