      "M": "Let E = {e1, e2, ..., en} be a set of comparable entities and let m be an attribute measured over time. Let v(e, m, t) denote the value of entity e for attribute m at time t.\n\nFor a shared interval [t_start, t_end], a growth divergence pattern holds when there exist two entities ei and ej such that both entities are measured using the same attribute, aggregation method, start time, and end time, and their absolute gap increases meaningfully over the interval:\n\n|v(ei, m, t_end) - v(ej, m, t_end)| > |v(ei, m, t_start) - v(ej, m, t_start)| + epsilon\n\nwhere epsilon >= 0 is a threshold for a meaningful increase in separation.\n\nEquivalently, define the pairwise gap between ei and ej at time t as:\n\ngap(ei, ej, m, t) = |v(ei, m, t) - v(ej, m, t)|\n\nThen growth divergence holds when:\n\ngap(ei, ej, m, t_end) - gap(ei, ej, m, t_start) > epsilon\n\nAll compared entities must use the same attribute, aggregation method, start time, and end time. Entities with missing start or end values cannot be used to establish the pattern.",
      "T": "A growth divergence pattern is a relational temporal pattern in which two comparable entities move farther apart over a shared time interval. It captures cases where one country, technology, organization, application area, or other category pulls away from another, or where one grows while the other remains flat or declines. The pattern requires a common measure and time interval for both entities, an explicit or implicit comparison between them, and tabular evidence that the difference between their values is larger at the end of the interval than at the beginning. A simple difference in CAGR, rank, final value, or absolute size is not sufficient unless it corresponds to a widening gap between the two time series.",
