          "prediction": "For a pair of entities (A, B) observed over a defined interval [Y₀, Y₁] the pattern holds when the two entities exhibit distinct annual growth rates and the difference in their patent‑family counts (the gap) changes measurably between the start and end of the interval. Formally, let C_A(Y) and C_B(Y) be the patent‑family counts for A and B in year Y. Define:\n\n- g_A = (C_A(Y₁)/C_A(Y₀))^{1/(Y₁‑Y₀)} ‑ 1  (annualized growth rate of A)\n- g_B = (C_B(Y₁)/C_B(Y₀))^{1/(Y₁‑Y₀)} ‑ 1  (annualized growth rate of B)\n- gap₀ = C_A(Y₀) ‑ C_B(Y₀)  (initial count difference)\n- gap₁ = C_A(Y₁) ‑ C_B(Y₁)  (final count difference)\n- Δgap = gap₁ ‑ gap₀  (change in the gap)\n\nThe comparative growth pattern is present when:\n1. g_A ≠ g_B (the entities grow at different rates),\n2. Δgap ≠ 0 (the count gap does not remain constant), and\n3. |Δgap| is substantively large relative to the magnitudes of C_A and C_B (e.g., exceeding a domain‑specific threshold or representing a clear divergence).\n\nIn practice the evidence tables provide the concrete values start_year, start_count, end_year, end_count, start_gap, end_gap, and gap_delta, which instantiate the above definitions. The pattern captures cases where one entity’s patent‑family portfolio outpaces the other’s over the observed period, as illustrated in the paired examples."
